<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golden Tara of Agusan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Agusan image</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golden Tara of Agusan is a gold statuette of a seated female deity found in 1917 on the bank of the Wawa river in Agusan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>, in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Probably made between the 9th and 13th centuries, it is variously identified as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> or Buddhist goddess and is evidence of Indic religious influence in the region of the Butuan kingdom. It is held by the Field Museum in Chicago.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-tara-of-agusan.jpg" alt="Golden Tara of Agusan" width="320" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Filippine,_provincia_di_agusan,_immagine_hindu,_statuetta_in_oro_massiccio,_xiii_secolo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
